WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.620
'Bohemian Rhapsody' won the Oscar for Best Editing at the 2019 Academy Awards.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:12.960
This is interesting because the movie contains several scenes that are masterclasses in poor editing.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.380
"Right. Now that everybody's got an acceptable name, let's get to it."

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.900
"Look, we just really need something special..."

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.300
"...more hits..."

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.820
"...like Killer Queen."

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.820
Let's examine one of the worst offenders in the film, and break down

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:32.300
...why exactly the editing of the scene is so bad.

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:42.980
The cuts in this scene are particularly jarring.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.420
They stand out to an attentive audience member for 3 reasons:

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.220
First, many of the cuts are unmotivated.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.600
Second, many ignore spatial continuity.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.020
And third, the pace is simply too fast.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.360
Let's deal with the easiest of these problems first.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.600
If a cut is unmotivated,

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:08.440
...another reason you might choose to cut is to create or maintain the pace of a scene, or to build tension.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.820
You can see this kind of effect during this scene from The Godfather,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.080
...where editor, Walter Murch, in a moment of silence,

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.440
...chooses to cut back and forth between these two characters.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:28.900
It's almost like the audience is anxiously looking back and forth between them, waiting to see who will speak.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:34.820
Using pacing to create tension or emotion, however, can't just be forced upon a scene through the edit.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.860
You've to work with and complement the performances and cinematography.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:45.180
And in fact, the faster the pace, the more attention must be paid to things like spatial continuity.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.840
Errors will simply lead to more difficulty following the action.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.760
The pace of this scene is incredible.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:02:01.080
In this 104-second scene, there are an astounding 60 cuts for an average shot length of 1.8 seconds.

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.540
To put this into perspective,

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:07.580
...this 136-second fight scene from Transformers: The Last Knight

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:13.080
...only has 49 cuts and an average shot length of 2.8 seconds.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:18.560
That's an action sequence from one of the most notoriously hyperactive directors out there.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.760
Compare that to the scene of some guys sitting around a table. That's over 30% faster.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.360
Absolutely nothing about this scene justifies or requires this kind of ridiculous pace.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.820
The rest of the film is fast-paced, and the pace fits a little bit better for the musical and concert sequences

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:43.180
I can see how they were possibly trying to keep the energy level high for the dialogue scenes as well,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.980
...but it just doesn't work.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:49.760
The pacing is the most obvious issue, but it's far from the only one.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.940
Let's examine some of these cuts in detail and the other two things that make the edits so jarring.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:59.720
The primary thing that motivates most cuts is the revelation of new information.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.440
Most important is emotional information.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:07.960
A large majority of the cuts in a dialogue-driven scene are so we can see a character's face,

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.800
...as they deliver a line or are reacting to somebody else's line.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:19.980
New information can also be geographic, to establish the character's relationship to each other spatially,

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.500
...or to let the audience see an action that's taking place.

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:28.660
Of course, a shot can contain multiple pieces of new information,

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:34.700
...but it's rarely a good idea to make a cut when that shot reveals no new information at all.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.380
Let's examine what motivates some of the cuts in this scene.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:47.660
"Wow."

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:50.340
"I didn't know it was fancy dress, Fred."

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:51.960
"I've gotta make an impression, darling."

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.680
"You look like an angry lizard."

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.880
The scene starts off decently.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:01.740
We get a nice shot that starts with Freddie Mercury coming through the doors, and pans into a wide.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.300
The second shot gives us a better view of Freddie approaching the table,

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:10.260
...and the third allows us to see the delivery of the first line by Brian May.

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.820
"I didn't know it was fancy dress, Fred."

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.420
Then we cut back to the shot of Freddie to see him showing off the outfit,

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:20.020
...and then to a close-up of his emotional reaction to Brian's line.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:24.100
We probably could have stayed on the line here, but jumping into the close-up isn't a huge issue.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:27.580
Then we cut to Roger Taylor's reaction to Brian's second line,

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:29.933
...and then back to Brian's reaction to Rogers laugh.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.960
We cut back to Freddy to see his laugh and the action of him sitting down,

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.140
...as well as John Reed entering in the background.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.800
So far, okay.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.700
This isn't Oscar-worthy editing, but the cuts reveal new information and makes sense.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:49.420
It's a little bit too fast, but it's establishing the nervous energy of the band as they wait for the meeting to begin,

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:53.840
...but this is where the scene begins to run into some major trouble. Let's watch.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:55.060
"Very subtle."

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:56.240
"You gonna fly away?"

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.880
"Can I borrow it for Sunday church?"

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.220
So here we cut to Brian's line,

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:13.680
...but then we cut back very quickly to see the rest of Freddy sitting down,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.740
...and then quickly back to Brian again.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:21.060
We already know Brian is making these wisecracks, so seeing him deliver the line isn't super important.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:27.040
We already know Freddy is sitting down, so returning to the shot of him sitting down is repetitive information,

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:29.500
...and his reaction is still the same as before.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.680
Either of these shots would likely be fine to hold on,

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:39.180
...but the quick back-and-forth creates the first sense of whiplash, that will only worsen as this scene progresses.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.140
On the shot of Brian, we see John Reed pass by.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:45.240
John grabbing the chair interrupts the band's casual chat.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:51.000
Following that shot, we have two reaction shots back-to-back: One from Brian, and one from Freddy,

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.860
...where they're both looking up towards where John Reed's face would be

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:59.700
However, since two shots have passed since we last saw John standing in that position,

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:03.020
...the eyeline of these reactions is a little bit awkward.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.760
Also, both of these reactions would be happening simultaneously.

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:12.340
It looks like the editor tries to make it feel more simultaneous by cutting quickly, but it doesn't sell.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:16.040
Now we get a shot of Reed that establishes the correct eyeline height,

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:21.260
...but his eyeline looks like it is directed at John Deacon and Roger Taylor.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:26.120
But instead of cutting to them, we cut back to Brian for a second reaction shot.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:30.140
We can even fix this fairly easily just by swapping these two shots.

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:35.640
Still not great, but it's a little bit better.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:40.260
After John Reed sits down, the eyeline is again completely ignored.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:45.480
While we've just cut from John and Roger, it now looks like he's looking at Freddie.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:50.740
Then Reed shifts his eyeline over to where John and Roger are sitting, and we cut to...

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:51.340
...Freddie.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:56.480
This section flows much better if we just remove some of the complexity.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.220
Here's the original edit:

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:08.660
"So this is Queen."

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:10.680
And here's my simplified cut:

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:17.280
"So this is Queen."

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:23.800
Again, it's not perfect, but if I can smooth things out just by shifting the existing shots around,

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:29.820
...there's no reason an editor with access to all the footage couldn't have cut a better scene.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.320
Let's look at the next section:

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.540
"So, this is Queen."

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:34.760
"And you..."

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:36.340
"...must be Freddie Mercury."

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:39.120
"You've got a gift. You all have."

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:45.260
"So tell me... What makes Queen any different from all the other wannabe rock stars I meet?"

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:49.860
After this line, "So this is Queen," we immediately run into another problem.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:55.860
Reed says, "So this is Queen," and we cut to a shot of exactly 3/4ths of Queen.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:59.260
It would make way more sense to show all four of them here.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:05.580
There's not anything that these three are doing that explains specifically cutting to just the three of them.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.180
We cut quickly back to Reed, but he's completely shifted in his seat.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:13.460
The character making this kind of movement off-camera is usually fine,

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:18.920
...but less than a second passes between these cuts, so it almost appears as a jump cut.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:26.353
Also, this spatial orientation of where he's pointing is confusing, coming off of this poorly-chosen wide shot.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:31.120
It would make a ton of sense to break up the pacing here with a longer shot of Freddie,

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:38.900
...but instead of holding on Freddie for a longer reaction, we cut back to a completely strange new angle of Reed.

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:43.820
This new angle gives us nothing by the way of new emotional or spatial information,

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:48.600
...and it just continues to add complexity to a scene that's already going by too quickly.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:52.760
Then we go back to this awkward wide that includes only three of the band members,

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:57.340
...presumably because there's no wide of all four band members to show all their reactions

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:02.840
The editor has to patch together an awkward combination of this wide and this close-up,

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:04.960
...which just doesn't gel at all.

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:10.920
The lack of a good wide including all four members of the band, I think, is a core difficulty with this scene,

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:16.340
...and it might be a clue that part of the blame here falls on the director or cinematographer

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:21.200
...for not getting proper coverage, or just blocking and composing the scene poorly.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.020
We continue to get reaction shots that are unnecessary.

00:09:25.020 --> 00:09:31.600
We've already seen this expression on these band members' faces in shots 22 and 24.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:36.140
We don't need to see it again, and this would have been a great chance to slow things down.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:43.660
I could continue to work through each cut in this scene, but many of the cuts have these same repeated issues.

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:45.500
One key offender sticks out:

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.760
Here, we cut to a wide shot where Paul magically appears beside John Reed,

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:55.960
...not to mention this jump to a super wide shot is completely unmotivated and doesn't make any sense.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:00.920
It's usually difficult to judge who's at fault when it comes to editing.

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:07.720
If an editor is given a scene that's poorly blocked and composed, it's difficult to fix that in the edit.

00:10:07.720 --> 00:10:13.480
However, it seems unlikely that cutting this scene in this way and choosing this pace

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:16.760
...was the best option the editor had with the scene.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:19.860
This is easily the worst edited scene in the film,

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:24.240
...but many of the dialogue scenes have these same issues this scene has.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.620
There are plenty of scenes with perfectly functional editing,

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:33.300
...although I'd be hard-pressed to find examples of the kind of editing you'd hope would earn an Academy Award.

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:39.320
There's also examples of awkward transitions, or montages that cover periods of touring,

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:43.400
...but do a poor job of conveying the necessary feeling.

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:47.580
So why did this film win an Academy Award for Best Editing?

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:50.580
Just like I mentioned, editing is hard to judge.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.280
Good editing should rarely be noticeable.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:56.120
Good editing tells the story effectively.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:11:01.500
And when a movie is edited well, you usually walk away from it thinking, "That was a great film,"

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.920
...not, "That editing was good."

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:10.639
So, counter-intuitively, the Oscar sometimes ends up going to films with the most noticeable editing,

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:13.860
...which, generally, is not the film with the best editing.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.401
There was a lot of turmoil during the production of this film,

00:11:16.401 --> 00:11:20.681
...so I can understand how this film maybe ended up how it did.

00:11:20.681 --> 00:11:26.561
It's easier to pick apart bad editing than it is to edit a scene that's not shot well,

00:11:26.561 --> 00:11:31.281
...but an Academy Award for Best Editing for this film is completely undeserved.

00:11:31.281 --> 00:11:35.361
An editing like this, ultimately, is disrespectful to the audience.

00:11:35.361 --> 00:11:41.281
It assumes your attention span is so short that your attention has to be held with quick, flashy cuts.

00:11:41.281 --> 00:11:44.740
Sometimes an editor just needs to step back, slow down,

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:51.680
...and trust that an audience member who's chosen to come see your film is interested and invested in the story.

